{
  "image_name": "0bc3d491e2fa706e14cfdb0416cdea12.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how extracellular vesicles (EVs), especially tumour-derived EVs, can be engineered and used for therapy. It combines three ideas: how cargo is loaded, how the EV surface is modified, and what biological effects these redesigned vesicles can achieve.\n\nOn the left, donor cells are shown producing EVs through endogenous loading pathways, where RNA and proteins are selectively packaged, or through overexpression of desired components. Cargo can also be added more directly using methods such as viral transduction, liposomes, electroporation, membrane permeabilization, or chemical loading. The central and right portions depict many surface-engineering strategies, including insertion of membrane proteins, attachment of antibodies or other ligands, fusion with liposomes, and creation of hybrid EV-like systems. These modifications allow EVs to carry miRNA, drugs, nanoparticles, or other hydrophilic cargo, and to present tumour antigens or targeting molecules on their surface.\n\nThe bottom row highlights the intended outcomes of these engineered EVs: stronger drug activity, improved uptake and lysosomal escape, targeting of tumours in different organs, immune modulation, longer circulation in blood, and use as tumour vaccines through dendritic-cell priming. Overall, the figure presents EVs as versatile, customizable delivery vehicles for cancer therapy.",
